  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 318 AM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places high stretching from southern\nSaskatchewan through North Dakota into western Minnesota, with\nlow over eastern Idaho/northern Utah. Upper level analysis\nplaces broad trough over the Upper Midwest with low over the\nPacific Northwest, while compact ridge noted ahead of the low\nover northern Wyoming into Montana. An upper level jet passing\nthrough is resulting in a few light rain showers over southern\nportions of North Dakota.\n\nFor today, aforementioned rain showers will diminish early this\nmorning. Thereafter, a mainly quiet day is expected as ridge\nahead of the aforementioned low passes over the area. In the\nmeantime, surface low pushes eastward over Wyoming/southern\nMontana resulting in increasing southeasterly winds in the\nafternoon, mainly over southwestern North Dakota.\n\nFor late tonight into Monday, rain develops and spreads over our\narea as the upper low moves quickly over the northern Rockies, \nthen along the international border, while surface low quickly \nlifts north/northeast and deepens over northwest North \nDakota/northeast Montana before sliding into southern\nSaskatchewan/Manitoba. This is a bit more southerly than we saw\ntrends heading towards yesterday, which is reflected in the \nlatest NBM forecast for 0.50 inch of rainfall or greater, which \nnow has the 50% or greater area north and east of a line from \nElgin to Beulah to Watford City, with areas around the Turtle \nMountains having a 50% chance of seeing over an inch. \nPrecipitation will then taper off in the morning into the early \nafternoon from southwest to northeast.\n\nAs the low lifts to the northeast and deepens, winds will shift\nwesterly and increase. The concern for strong wind gusts on\nMonday has also been on the increase, as the latest ECMWF EFI \nnow has bumped up to 0.9 over a broad swath of western into \nparts of central North Dakota, an increase of 0.1 to 0.2 from \nyesterday. Therefore, will have to take a second look at this \nlater today to make sure this trend sticks, with possible wind \nheadlines being issued. \n\nWinds gradually diminish Monday night, though look\nto remain on the breezy side over central and northwest North\nDakota on Tuesday. Other than the winds, a quiet day is expected\non Tuesday. Thereafter, a pattern sets up with a variety of\ndisturbances moving through, bringing occasional low to medium\nprecipitation chances. A modest warming trend is also expected.",
